<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/advanced/exoplanets-and-detection">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    In 1991 the textbook answer was: <strong>we have no evidence for planets outside the solar
    system</strong>. Today the count exceeds 5,800 confirmed worlds — hot Jupiters, super-Earths,
    mini-Neptunes, systems with six planets in resonant clockwork. Most were found not by seeing
    them but by <strong>watching their stars for dips and wobbles</strong> — astronomy's
    indirect-detection masterpiece.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="how a dot of starlight betrays an invisible world" widget="equationStep" config={transitCfg} />

  <h2>What the harvest taught us</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Our system is not the template.</strong> The easiest planets to find (huge, close-in "hot Jupiters") are rare and unlike anything home-grown. Systems are diverse; the solar system is one configuration among many.</li>
    <li><strong>Rocky worlds are common:</strong> estimates suggest most stars host at least one planet of Earth's size — tens of billions in the Milky Way alone.</li>
    <li><strong>The habitable zone:</strong> the orbital band where liquid water survives — Earth sits in the Sun's; TRAPPIST-1 hosts three Earth-sized planets in its red dwarf's. Zone location is necessary, not sufficient (atmospheres decide).</li>
    <li><strong>The next prize: biosignatures.</strong> Detecting oxygen + methane together in an Earth twin's atmosphere would be strong evidence of life. JWST takes the first readings; future 30-metre telescopes and proposed space interferometers aim directly at it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Mayor & Queloz (1995, Nobel 2019); NASA Exoplanet Archive; JWST results (2022–).</p>
</TopicPage>
